15:17:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I appreciate you coming back with your first‑hand experience—that’s a completely fair observation, and you’re right to question it. The feeling of a floor doesn’t just come down to the material; it’s the *engineering* of that material. Let me tackle the two countries separately, because they work very differently. ### 1. Spain: You’re right, they were probably concrete In most of Spain, single-family houses (and even many low-rise apartments) are built with reinforced concrete frames and beam‑and‑block or solid concrete slabs at every level, including the upper floor. That’s the Mediterranean heavy construction tradition. It provides thermal mass, excellent sound insulation, and a completely rigid feel. So your memory of a rock‑solid upper floor there is almost certainly genuine. In that context, the UK’s timber floor would be the outlier. ### 2. United States: Very likely timber, but engineered to feel totally rigid This is the crucial bit. The standard upper floor in a US wood‑frame house *is* a timber‑joist floor, not concrete. So why didn’t it bounce like a British floor? Because US practice incorporates a package of details that make an enormous difference: **– Deeper, stronger, more frequent joists** US residential joists are often deeper (2×10 or 2×12, i.e., 9¼"–11¼" deep) spaced 16 inches on center (about 400 mm, but often closer). UK practice historically used shallower joists (e.g., 7" or 8" deep) at wider centres (up to 600 mm in older homes). Deeper and more frequent joists dramatically increase stiffness. **– Engineered I‑joists** The vast majority of modern US homes use silent‑floor I‑joists (TJIs) designed to L/480 deflection criteria, sometimes L/600 for tile. That’s much stiffer than the minimum L/360 that older solid‑sawn joists might provide, let alone some undersized UK heritage floors. **– Glued and screwed subfloor** The standard is ¾" (19 mm) tongue‑and‑groove OSB or plywood, glued along the joist tops with construction adhesive and screwed/nailed. This essentially creates a single stressed‑skin panel that ties the joists together. It eliminates squeaks and vastly reduces point‑load deflection. The UK historic floor of square‑edged floorboards nailed imperfectly to joists cannot match that. **– Underlayment and finish floors** Often there’s a second layer of thin plywood, then thick carpet pad, or a layer of cement board for tile, adding mass and damping. The whole composite feels like one solid slab. **– Continuous structural design** The floor diaphragm is connected to shear walls, blocking, and bridging, so when you walk across it, the load is shared across many joists, not just one or two. This makes deflection almost imperceptible. So, your experience of a "solid, rigid" US floor is completely consistent with a timber floor built to those standards. It feels like concrete, even though it isn’t. A properly designed and installed modern timber floor will not bounce—it will meet strict deflection limits that make it feel perfectly firm. ### Why many traditional UK timber floors *do* bounce - Older UK houses (pre‑1950s) often had undersized, poor‑quality softwood joists, sometimes just 4"×2" (100×50 mm) spanning 12–14 feet. - Floorboards were plain‑edged, nailed down loose, and shrunk over time, leading to movement, squeaks, and a trampoline feel. - Modern mass‑housebuilding in the UK has sometimes pushed minimum‑spec joists and chipboard to save cost, giving a slightly springier feel even in new homes. But today, a UK upper floor built with proper joist sizing (e.g., C24 timber or engineered joists), glued 22 mm chipboard, and solid blocking will also feel very rigid. It’s just that the US went further, earlier, in standardising the “glued‑screwed monolithic diaphragm” approach. **Bottom line:** Your senses didn’t lie. In Spain, you walked on concrete. In the US, you walked on a very stiff timber floor system that’s designed to mimic that solid feel. The UK has historically been slack in that regard, which is why you associate timber with bounce. But it’s a design and detailing issue, not the mere presence of wood.
